//
// A stage: one Bund VM on its own thread, between two channels of the
// local bus. It takes a value from its inbox, runs `step` on it, and sends
// what `step` leaves to its outbox, until it takes the string "stop".
//
// The host defines, before this file runs:
//
//   :name     what the stage is called, for its tally
//   :inbox    the channel it reads
//   :outbox   the channel it writes
//   :tally    the channel it reports its count on when it stops
//   step      the work: one value in, one value out
//
// A `recv` on a channel nothing has created is an error, not NODATA, and
// under several VMs no program order says who creates it first. `bus.data`
// creates the channel it asks about, so every stage creates its own inbox
// before it reads it.
//
// `recv` does not wait: an empty channel answers NODATA (type 97), and the
// stage yields its thread with `0 sleep` and asks again. When several
// stages share one inbox, each value goes to exactly one of them, and the
// others are answered NODATA.
//
// On "stop" the stage reports how many values it handled, then passes the
// "stop" on, so the stage after it stops too.
//

:inbox var? bus.data drop
:handled 0 var

{
  :inbox var? recv
  type.of 97 ==
  {
    type.of 4 ==
    {
      step
      :outbox var? swap send.quick
      :handled :handled var? 1 + var
      true
    }
    {
      :tally var?
        dict :name :name var? set :handled :handled var? set
        send.quick
      :outbox var? swap send.quick
      false
    }
    ifthenelse
  }
  { drop 0 sleep true }
  ifthenelse
} for
